#!/usr/bin/env python3
# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""
AI Web Bridge 7  -  one file, double-click to run.

Turns the chat websites you are logged into into a local, standard API for
Claude Code (Anthropic /v1/messages) and for Cursor / VS Code agents / anything
OpenAI-compatible (/v1/chat/completions), with a web dashboard for accounts and keys.

HOW IT WORKS (no per-site hard-coding)
  1. ADD ACCOUNT  - a real browser opens; you log in once (the old way).
  2. LEARN        - the bridge chats with the site through its own UI while recording the
                    network traffic, then reverse-engineers a "recipe": endpoint, body template,
                    auth source, which fields are per-chat / per-turn state, and how the answer
                    is streamed. Recipes are verified by replaying them.
  3. RUN          - tier DIRECT: plain HTTPS straight to the site's server (no browser, no RAM).
                    tier PAGE : one in-page fetch() (tiny headless page) when direct is refused.
                    tier UI   : drives the page headlessly only when nothing else verifies.
                    The tier is chosen by verification and downgraded automatically on failure.
  4. REMEMBER     - every learned site gets docs/<host>.md and tools/<host>_client.py, so the
                    next login (any account on that site) skips the browser entirely.

PERSONAL CHROME (7.24)
  Browser-dependent steps (login, learning, token refresh, PAGE and UI tiers) now run in YOUR OWN Chrome through a small Manifest-V3 extension that this file carries inside itself
  (extracted to <data>/personal_chrome/extension, verified by SHA-256). Chrome never installs extensions silently, so there is ONE human step: chrome://extensions > Developer mode >
  Load unpacked > pick that folder, then pair with the one-time code from `python bridge.py --pc-pair`. The bridge opens its own task tabs (it never navigates or closes your other tabs),
  talks to the extension over an authenticated loopback channel (pinned extension origin + token), and Chrome shows its own "is debugging this browser" bar while a tab is inspected.
  `python bridge.py --pc-status` prints what is and is not equivalent to the old Playwright browser. BRIDGE_BROWSER=playwright selects the old separate browser explicitly; nothing falls back to it silently.

ERRORS, LIMITS, RETRIES (7.5)
  Every failure is classified (pure function classify_error; HTTP status / error kind first, wording second in English and Persian, anything
  unclear = `unknown` with low confidence, never "quota") into: category, scope (model/account/provider/network/request), retryable,
  failover_ok, auth_implicated, limit_confirmed, may_have_been_accepted, partial_output_sent, confidence, redacted evidence. It is returned as
  `x_bridge.error` in the error body / stream error event and as x-bridge-error-category/-scope/-retryable headers. A site's own error shapes
  can be declared as data in its profile (`error_map`, validated).
  Limits: a model-scoped or unclear rate limit rests ONE route (short when unclear); an account-wide one or a usage cap rests the account;
  the site's Retry-After / "try again in 5 minutes" wins, otherwise a configured default (cooldown_ambiguous / cooldown_default / cooldown_usage_cap)
  - the bridge never invents a quota and never walks through sibling models hoping one works.
  Retries: one budget per request (retry_attempts, retry_deadline_seconds). Another eligible route is tried at once; with none, a transient error is
  retried on the same route at most retry_same_route times with exponential backoff + jitter, only if the request provably did not reach the site.
  Invalid-request / blocked-content / unsupported / too-large errors are never retried or failed over. Per-route circuit breaker (closed -> open ->
  half-open, ONE probe, doubling open time): only health failures count. Re-learning a site needs 3 consecutive unparseable answers; network blips never count.

TASK STATE, SAFE FAILOVER, HANDOFF (7.6)
  Failover only at safe boundaries: after any byte reached the client the stream is ended with the protocol's error event (never restarted on another
  route and spliced). If a request MAY have been processed (timeout, empty answer, lost response) the bridge never resends it on the same route - not
  even on a lower tier - and either lets ANOTHER route try it (uncertain_acceptance = "other_route", default, flagged in the decision) or stops with an
  explicit "outcome uncertain" error ("stop"): x_bridge.error.outcome_uncertain + x-bridge-outcome: uncertain. No site is asked to reconcile yet.
  Canonical task state: tasks/<task_id>.json (task id = hash of key + system + first user message, independent of account/route/model): full-content
  message hashes with stable ids, divergence records (edit/rewind/reset), tool-call lifecycle requested -> delivered -> result_received -> validated ->
  completed | failed | unknown, files named in tool arguments and result lines as reported by tool results, route history, failed attempts, checkpoints.
  Credential-looking strings are masked in stored copies; the file is bounded (task_file_max_kb, task_max, task_calls_max) and written atomically.
  GET /api/tasks and /api/tasks/<id> (admin). A failure in this bookkeeping never breaks serving; task_state=false stores nothing.
  Handoff: a route that has not served the task yet gets a derived <handoff> note (open/uncertain tool calls, completed count, paths, result lines),
  labelled as bridge-derived and neutralised as untrusted. Tool-call ids: a retry of the same request that yields the same logical call keeps the id the
  client may already hold; a failed write marks the call `unknown`, never `delivered`.

CONTEXT (7.7, group E). The prompt is built by one engine: true duplicates -> type-aware reduction of big tool results (json/diff/log/listing/search/text; the full text stays
  in vault/<hash>.json, reference in the note; JSON is never cut mid-structure) -> deterministic digest of old turns (extracted from the transcript, no extra upstream
  request) -> explicit context_too_large (HTTP 413). Never cut: system prompt, tool schemas, tool-call arguments, the latest message. Every reduction is recorded:
  GET /api/context (admin; no prompt text, measured savings with the method stated), GET /api/vault/<ref> (admin). Remote chats carry a trust level
  (verified|observed|uncertain|broken), survive a restart (continuity.json, bounded) and are rebuilt locally after repeated rejections. Settings: fixed_soft_chars,
  context_hard_chars, digest_chars, vault_*, continuity_*. Self-test: python bridge.py --selftest [--quick].

TEXT-FIRST SUBMISSION, SIGNATURES, WINDOW BUDGET (7.8)
  The bridge always tries to send ordinary text. When a site's paste limit is known (paste_limit_chars, recipe.long_paste.limit_chars, or learned from what the SITE
  itself reported: recipe.long_paste.signal = {path, kind: attachment|truncated} read from the response, or an HTTP 413) the prompt is fitted to it: paste_policy =
  reduce (default: the E3/E4 ladder with a smaller budget) | split (ordered text parts at paragraph boundaries, ONLY if recipe.long_paste.split is true; each part is a
  real extra request and is counted) | attach (let the site convert it; counted, capped by paste_uploads_max per hour, explicit error beyond) | error. The decision is in
  GET /api/context -> recent[].submission. Nothing is dropped silently. Message signatures are canonical, typed full-content hashes (text/tool_use/tool_result/image/
  thinking). A route's declared context window (routing.json context_windows, tokens) now sets the prompt budget instead of only excluding the route.
  7.14: group O governing rules + O1 (mock only): explicit persisted account lifecycle (adding/logging_in/capturing/learning/validating/stable_direct/degraded_fallback/repairing/needs_login/needs_human/disabled)
  with reasons, timestamps, an event list and restart recovery; stable_direct only from validating with a validation id (O8 does not exist yet, so nothing is stable); needs_login/needs_human make a route
  ineligible; learning budget (learn_budget_requests=12, repair_budget_requests=3) counted per account and shown on the card; credential guards (O16) and the extract-never-guess manifest (O20).
  7.21: group O step 17 (mock only; no real site or browser run): O8 validation battery + a minimal native client (request from the O18 modules, O19 identity headers, session sources, stream decode, typed errors,
  cancel), 7 scenarios + the same battery after a session refresh, parity with the page tier, promotion only after >= 2 independent runs separated by a gap (lc_transition with a validation id), backoff, persisted state.
  7.22: group O step 18 = O9 (mock only; no real site or browser run): direct session renewal (`O9Refresher`: the captured refresh request replayed by the native transport with the identity headers and stored cookies;
  placeholders instead of a stored body or secret; 401/403 = needs_login, 429/5xx/network = transient and changes nothing), a single door to the browser (`O9Browsers`: only for refresh / recover_login / repair, a budget, idle timer,
  closes at once after the job), measured process-table helpers (`o9_measure`), the needs_login card action, and the repair loop (`o9_on_drift`, `o9_repair_tick`: drift -> degraded_fallback -> bounded re-learn with backoff -> O8 -> stable_direct).
  7.20: group O step 16 (mock only; no real site or browser run): O19 browser identity of the owner's own login (UA, Accept*, sec-ch-ua*, sec-fetch-*, language order) stored as data in
  site_modules/<host>/identity.py (AST allow-list, hash pin, manifest, last-known-good) and replayed with the same header set and order via protocol.build_headers; never invented, mixed, rotated or randomised.
  7.16: group O steps 9-12 (real headless Chromium + a local mock site; no real site): O4 credential-free capture (CDP) with a hard model boundary, O22 total network analysis,
  O21 per-site source analysis (acorn via a local, pinned, consented toolchain; low-confidence path without it), O23 other surfaces, analysis/<host>/report.md, --analyze.
  7.15: group O steps 5-8 (mock only; no real site or browser run): O2 add-account input normalised (normalize_site_input; POST /api/sites/normalize; hint + live preview), O10 portable data folder (MANIFEST.json,
  per-account session check valid/expired/looks IP-device-bound/needs_login via ONE cheap non-consuming probe, --doctor, --export-bundle/--import-bundle, 0700/0600), O3 login accepted only on >= 2 of 4 signals held twice
  (or the owner's 'I'm done'; LoginWatcher) + clean abort when the window is closed early, O7 CAPTCHA/verification/MFA => needs_human (routing skips at once; the owner solves it; never solved by the bridge).
  7.13: dashboard panel "Context & submission" (H, part of E7): GET /api/context shown read-only with the labels measured / estimated / learned, learned route windows, recent submission decisions
  and canary results; a confirm()-guarded button runs POST /api/accounts/<id>/paste-discover (3 real requests, counted). Opening the dashboard sends no model request; CSP unchanged.
  7.10: E1 canary (opt-in, config continuity_canary / continuity_canary_every): a fresh remote chat gets a bridge marker (no extra request); every N continued turns ONE
  extra, counted request asks the chat for it before the real turn. Missing = silent reset -> that chat is rebuilt locally with the full history (never a context-less delta).
  7.9: source code is its own result type (outline: imports + definition signatures thinned evenly + task-term lines, ABSOLUTE line numbers in the omission
  markers); GET /api/vault/<ref>?lines=A-B returns exactly that slice (capped by vault_slice_max_lines); tool names/descriptions/schemas are neutralised like other
  third-party text; pluggable token estimator (register_tokenizer + config `tokenizer`, safe fallback) used for BOTH the prompt budget and route eligibility.

ROUTING (7.4)
  A route is (account, model variant). Which route serves a request is decided by a policy (data in bridge_data/routing.json,
  validated on save): fixed, auto, rotation, failover, lowest_latency, best_fit, manual_approval. Resolution of the `model` a client
  sends, in this order (exact matching only, never a guess; unknown = 404, ambiguous = 400 naming the exact routes to use):
  alias / bridge-pool/<name>  >  acct:<id>[/model:<variant id>]  >  exact account id / label / host  >  exact variant id (only if ONE
  account offers it)  >  bridge-auto / bridge-fast / bridge-balanced / bridge-quality  >  client-model rules  >  client_model_prefixes.
  Names that come from a remote site (variant ids) never shadow a name the owner chose, and a key pinned to one account cannot see or
  reach anything outside it. Client-model rules are DATA (routing.json -> client_models, ordered, first match wins, case-insensitive):
      {"match": "prefix" | "suffix" | "contains" | "exact", "value": "<text>", "to": "bridge-fast" | "bridge-pool/<name>" | "<alias>" | "acct:<id>[/model:<v>]"}
  so any client's own model names (Claude Code sends real-looking ones, and a small/fast one for background work) can be mapped to a
  policy or profile without putting a provider's model name in this file. Out of the box the legacy `claude-` prefix means bridge-auto.
  After the resolution above, the policy that applies is decided by: API-key policy  >  account policy  >  global default.
  GET /v1/models lists what the calling key may select (routes, aliases, pools, selectable model variants) with an `x_bridge`
  extension (kind, status, confidence, stale, capabilities as supported/unsupported/unknown, native id); ?limit / after_id / before_id
  follow the Anthropic paging rules. A recipe may carry `identity: {"path": ...}`: the response field in which the SITE declares the
  model that answered; it is recorded as observed identity (in the response or after it) and nothing else ever is.
  bridge-fast / bridge-balanced / bridge-quality only change the execution profile (ranking weights, bounded; they never override a
  pin, an account restriction or a capability requirement). Every candidate is checked for eligibility first; each exclusion carries a
  reason code (see EXCLUSION_CODES) and "no eligible route" lists them all. Every request leaves a RouteDecision (GET /api/decisions)
  and x-bridge-route / -account / -model / -fallback / -policy / -profile / -model-identity headers. Model identity is three separate
  fields (requested, selected route, observed) and "observed" is only what the site/provider declared, never inferred from reply text.
  Admin API: GET/POST /api/routing, POST /api/routing/validate|preview|approve, POST /api/accounts/<id>/enabled, POST /api/accounts/<id>/paste-discover (E6: 3 real probe requests, counted).
  7.28: GET /api/o12 (admin, read-only): per browser account the lifecycle state, O8 validation record, O9 repair record, owner card (`card_action`) and the supervisor's last tick row; shown in the dashboard card "Direct path & validation" (a button only for the known card kinds relogin / relearn).

The bridge keeps running when Claude Code exits. Close the console to stop it.
Data lives in ./bridge_data (config, accounts, sessions, recipes, docs, tools, browser profiles).
"""
